              </thead>
              <tbody class="text-slate-700">
                {
                  gridAreas.map((a, index) => (
                    <tr class={index === gridAreas.length - 1 ? "" : "border-b border-slate-100"}>
                      <td class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold text-slate-900">
                        {a.toLocaleString("en-US")} ft²
                      </td>
                      {gridRates.map((r) => (
                        <td class="py-3 px-5">{fmt(roofRunoffGpm(a, r), 1)}</td>
                      ))}
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h2 class="mt-14 text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900">
            Horizontal drain capacity — hydraulic check
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
            Manning's equation for Schedule 40 flowing full. <strong>This is a sanity check, not the code
            answer</strong> — IPC Table 1106.3 governs the actual size.
          </p>

          <div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-sm">
            <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
              <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-slate-200 text-slate-500 bg-slate-50">
                  <th class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold">Size</th>
                  <th class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold">Slope</th>
                  <th class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold">Capacity flowing full</th>
                  <th class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold">Velocity</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="text-slate-700">
                {
                  capacityTable.map((row, index) => (
                    <tr class={index === capacityTable.length - 1 ? "" : "border-b border-slate-100"}>
                      <td class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold text-slate-900">{sizeLabel(row.nominal)}</td>
                      <td class="py-3 px-5">{row.slope} in/ft</td>
                      <td class="py-3 px-5">{fmt(row.gpm, 0)} gpm</td>
                      <td class="py-3 px-5">{fmt(row.velocity, 2)} ft/s</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-slate-500">
            Sources &amp; standards: the runoff conversion is derived from 7.48052 gallons per cubic foot, not
            quoted from a table. Design rainfall comes from the 100-year hourly rate — <a
              class="underline hover:text-sky-600"
              href="https://codes.iccsafe.org/codes/i-codes"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener">IPC</a
            > Figure 1106.1 and <a
              class="underline hover:text-sky-600"
              href="https://hdsc.nws.noaa.gov/pfds/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener">NOAA precipitation frequency data</a
            >. <strong>Pipe sizing is governed by IPC Tables 1106.2 and 1106.3, which are deliberately not
            reproduced here</strong>; the capacity column is Manning's equation as a hydraulic sanity check
            only. Secondary (overflow) drainage under IPC 1107 is a separate requirement. Roughly fifteen
            states use the <a
              class="underline hover:text-sky-600"
              href="https://www.iapmo.org/uniform-plumbing-code"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener">UPC</a
            > or a derivative. Local amendments override the model code.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

